@meta
id: 08-19
title: Scenario — the legacy refund path
chapter: 08
type: scenario
skills: delete-d, change-c, put-p, substitute-s, find-f
par: 112
minutes: 12
vscode: two tabs, a dozen double-clicks and a lot of retyping
open: order.provider.ts order.provider.test.ts default.json
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-527  cancel() still goes through the retired refundLegacy call,
             logs the whole order on every request, and writes a status
             the enum does not have. The test has a copy-pasted
             assertion that was meant to check the cancellation email.

Three files are open. `:bn` moves to the next one and `:bp` back — that is
chapter 16's material and you are borrowing it early, so do not go
hunting for more. Finish the whole job with `:wqa`, which writes every
buffer and quits.

  1. order.provider.ts

     - The imports are the wrong way round. Local modules come before
       infrastructure ones here.
     - `findFirst` is `findOne`. The rest of the chain is correct.
     - The `console.log` line goes.
     - The refund call is now `refund(order.id)` — the gateway works out
       the amount itself. Only the tail of that line changes.
     - The status is `CANCELLED`.
     - `legacyRefund` has no callers left. Remove the method and the
       blank line that separates it from `cancel`.

  2. order.provider.test.ts

     - The second `payment.refund` assertion is a copy-paste. It should
       be checking `email.cancelled`, with the same argument.
     - The expected status is wrong here too.

  3. default.json — open for reference, so you can see the codes exist.
     Change it and the check fails.

Nothing in either file needs retyping wholesale. If you find yourself
typing an import path or a whole line that already exists, stop and use
the register.

@keys
| `ddp` | move this line below the next one |
| `cw` | change to the end of the word |
| `C` | change from the cursor to the end of the line |
| `s` | substitute one character, then insert |
| `6dd` | delete six lines |
| `0` then `fD` | column zero, then forward to the `D` |
| `:bn` `:bp` | next buffer, previous buffer |
| `:wqa` | write every buffer and quit |

@hints
Take the provider top to bottom in one pass. Every edit in it is a verb
you learned in this chapter, and none of them needs more than four keys
of aiming.
--
The refund line is the only one where you retype anything, and only the
part after `refundLegacy`. Land on the `L` and change to the end of the
line.
--
`CANCELLD` is missing one letter in the middle. `r` cannot add a
character, but `s` on the final `D` lets you type `ED` in its place. The
same fix works in the test. `j` keeps whatever column you were in, so
press `0` before you go hunting along a line with `f`.
--
In the test, the duplicate line is not rubbish — it is the shape of the
assertion you need. Change `payment` to `email` and `refund` to
`cancelled` and it is the line the ticket asked for.

@solution
**order.provider.ts**

    ddp                          imports the right way round
    4j f.;l cwfindOne<Esc>       findFirst becomes findOne
    j dd                         the debug log
    j fL C(order.id);<Esc>       the tail of the refund call
    4j 0fD sED<Esc>             CANCELLD becomes CANCELLED
    3j 6dd                       the dead method and its blank line

**order.provider.test.ts** — `:bn`

    5j f(l cwemail<Esc>          payment becomes email
    2l cwcancelled<Esc>          refund becomes cancelled
    3j 0fD sED<Esc>             the status again

Then `:wqa`.

Around ninety keystrokes for eleven separate corrections across two
files, and the only text you typed that was not already in the repository
was `findOne`, `(order.id);`, `email`, `cancelled` and two `ED`s.

Three things this scenario is really testing.

Whether you reach for the register instead of the keyboard. The duplicate
assertion was a gift: the line you needed was already there, one word
away. People who have not internalised that still delete it and type a
new one.

Whether you pick the verb that fits the shape of the problem — `cw` for a
word, `C` for a tail, `s` for one character becoming two, `dd` with a
count for a block. Every one of those could be done with `x` and a lot of
patience, and the difference between the two is about a minute per file,
which is an hour a week.

And whether you go top to bottom. Every edit here was below the last one,
so the cursor only ever travelled forwards. Reordering your own work so
the cursor moves in one direction is a real habit, and it is most of what
separates a fast vim user from a fluent one.

@start order.provider.ts
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findFirst({ where: { id } });
    console.log('cancel', order);

    await this.paymentService.refundLegacy(order.id, order.total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLD' },
    });
  }

  public async legacyRefund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findFirst({ where: { id } });

    return this.paymentService.refundLegacy(order.id, order.total);
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@start order.provider.test.ts
describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  it('cancels an order', async () => {
    await provider.cancel('ord_1042');

    expect(payment.refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith('ord_1042');
    expect(payment.refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith('ord_1042');
    expect(repository.update).toHaveBeenCalledWith({
      where: { id: 'ord_1042' },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLD' },
    });
  });
});

@expected order.provider.test.ts
describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  it('cancels an order', async () => {
    await provider.cancel('ord_1042');

    expect(payment.refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith('ord_1042');
    expect(email.cancelled).toHaveBeenCalledWith('ord_1042');
    expect(repository.update).toHaveBeenCalledWith({
      where: { id: 'ord_1042' },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  });
});

